> **Q1.** How to (formally) prove the newly established theorem (A4)?

**A.** Following the reviewer’s suggestion, we provide the revised version of the statement and proof of Theorem 3.3.

**Theorem 3.3**
Suppose that $\mu$ and $
u$ are probability densities defined on $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$. 
Given the assumptions in Appendix A, suppose that $\mu,
u$ are absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure and $\Psi^*$ is continuously differentiable. Assuming that the optimal potential $v^\star = \inf_{v\in\mathcal{C}}J(v)$ exists, $v^\star$ is a solution of the following objective $$	ilde{J}(v) =  \int_{\mathcal{X}}{-v^c(x)d	ilde{\mu}(x)} +\int_{\mathcal{Y}}{-v(y)d	ilde{
u}(y)}$$
where $	ilde{\mu}(x)={\Psi_1^*}'(-{v^\star}^c(x))\mu(x)$ and $	ilde{
u}(y)={\Psi_2^*}'(-{v^\star}(y))
u(y)$. Note that the assumptions guarantee the existence of optimal transport map $T^\star$ between $	ilde{\mu}$ and $	ilde{
u}$. Furthermore, $T^\star$ satisfies 
$$T^\star (x)\in\arg\inf_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\left[c(x,y)-v^\star(y)ight],$$ $\mu-a.e.$. In particular, $D_{\Psi_1}(	ilde{\mu}|\mu)+D_{\Psi_2}(	ilde{
u}|
u)\leq	au\mathcal{W}_2^2(\mu,
u)$ where $\mathcal{W}_2(\mu,
u)$ is a Wasserstein-2 distance between $\mu$ and $
u$.

$ $

**proof.** 
For any $(f,g)\in\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{X})	imes \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Y})$, let $$I(f,g)=\int_{\mathcal{X}}-\Psi_1^* (-f(x))d\mu(x)+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}-\Psi_2^* (-g(y))d
u(y).$$Then, for arbitrarily given potentials $(u,v)\in\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{X})	imes\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Y})$, and perturbation $(\eta,\zeta)\in\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{X})	imes\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Y})$ which satisfies $u(x)+\eta(x)+v(y)+\zeta(y)\leq c(x,y)$,$$\mathcal{L}(\epsilon):= rac{d}{d\epsilon}I(u+\epsilon\eta,v+\epsilon\zeta)=\int_{\mathcal{X}}\eta{\Psi_1^*}'(-\left((u+\epsilon \eta)(x)ight))d\mu(x)+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}\zeta {\Psi_2^*}'(-\left((v+\epsilon\zeta)(y)ight))d
u(y).$$Note that $\mathcal{L}(\epsilon)$ is a continuous function. Thus, the functional derivative of $I(u,v)$, i.e. $\mathcal{L}(0)$, is given by calculus of variation (Chapter 8, Evans [85])$$\int_{\mathcal{X}}\delta u(x){\Psi_1^*}'(-u(x))d\mu(x)+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}\delta v(y){\Psi_2^*}'(-v(y))d
u(y),$$where $(\delta u,\delta v)\in\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{X})	imes \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Y})$ denote the variations of $(u,v)$, respectively. Note that such linearization must satisfy the inequality constraint $u(x)+\delta u(x)+v(y)+\delta v(y)\leq c(x,y)$ to give admissible variations. Then, the linearized dual form can be re-written as follows:$$\sup_{(u,v)\in\mathcal{C(\mathcal{X})}	imes\mathcal{C(\mathcal{Y})}}\int_X \delta u(x)d	ilde{\mu}(x)+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}\delta v(y)d	ilde{
u}(y),\qquad\qquad (a)$$where $\delta u(x)+\delta v(y)\leq c(x,y)-u(x)-v(y)$, $	ilde{\mu}(x)={\Psi_1^*}'(-u(x))\mu(x)$, and $	ilde{
u}(y)={\Psi_2^*}'(-v(y))
u(y)$. Thus, whenever $(u^\star,v^\star)$ is optimal, the linearized dual problem is non-positive due to the inequality constraint and tightness (i.e. $c(x,y)-u^\star (x)-v^\star (y)=0$ $\pi$-almost everywhere), which implies that Eq (a) achieves its optimum at $(u^\star,v^\star)$. Note that Eq (a) is a linearized dual problem of the following dual problem:$$\sup_{u(x)+v(y)\leq c(x,y)}\left[\int_{\mathcal{X}}u(x)d	ilde{\mu}(x)+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}v(y)d	ilde{
u}(y)ight].$$Hence, the UOT problem can be reduced into standard OT formulation with marginals $	ilde{\mu}$ and $	ilde{
u}$. Moreover, the continuity of ${\Psi^*}'$ gives that $	ilde{\mu},	ilde{
u}$ are absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure and have a finite second moment (Note that $\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}$ are compact. Hence, ${\Psi^*}'$ is bounded on $\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}$, and $\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}$ are bounded). By Theorem 2.12 in Villani et al. [79], there exists a unique measurable OT map $T^\star$ which solves Monge problem between $	ilde{\mu}$ and $	ilde{
u}$. Furthermore, as shown in Remark 5.13 in Villani et al. [71], $T^\star(x)\in{m{arginf}}_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\left[c(x,y)-v(y)ight]$ for $\mu-a.e.$.

Finally, if we constrain $\pi$ into $\Pi(\mu,
u)$, then the UOT objective $C_{ub}$ becomes $C_{ub}=	au \mathcal{W}^2 (\mu,
u)$. Thus, for the optimal $\pi \in \mathcal{M}+$ where $\pi_0=	ilde{\mu}$ and $\pi_1=	ilde{
u}$, it is trivial that$$	au\mathcal{W}^2 (\mu,
u)\geq	au\mathcal{W}^2(	ilde{\mu},	ilde{
u})+D_{\Psi_1}(	ilde{\mu}|\mu)+D_{\Psi_2}(	ilde{
u}|
u),$$which proves the last statement of the theorem.

